P(X = k) = \binom{n}{k} p^k (1-p)^{n-k}
\] where \( n \) is the number of trials (5 in this case), \( k \) is the number of successes (3 in this case), and \( p \) is the probability of success on a single trial (\( \frac{1}{3} \) in this case). Plugging in these values, we get: \[
P(X = 3) = \binom{5}{3} \left(\frac{1}{3}\right)^3 \left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^2 = 10 \left(\frac{1}{27}\right) \left(\frac{4}{9}\right) = \frac{40}{243}
\] Therefore, the probability that exactly 3 of the 5 rolls are either a 1 or a 2 is \( \frac{40}{243} \). </think> <answer> \(\frac{40}{243}\) </answer>

